course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 10
title: The colours, and two kinds of blue
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In English «blue» is one colour with shades; in Ukrainian there are two everyday blues with their own names: SYniy, dark blue like jeans or a deep sea, and blakYtnyi, light sky blue.
explain: It is a bit like the way English treats red and pink as two colours, though pink is only a light red.
explain: The Ukrainian flag shows both ideas: the law describes its colours as SYniy and ZHOvtyi, blue and yellow, but people very often call it zhovto-blakYtnyi, yellow and sky blue.
explain: Drawing at Sashko's kitchen table, his little sister asks yakoho kOlyoru? — what colour? — and the answers are words you already half know: zelEnyi green, BIlyi white, CHOrnyi black, SIryi grey.
fact: SYniy means dark blue; Ukrainian has two everyday blues, SYniy (dark) and blakYtnyi (light).
fact: blakYtnyi means light blue or sky blue; calling the sky SYniy is not wrong, but it sounds dark.
fact: ZHOvtyi means yellow, the colour of sunflowers, the sun and the flag's lower half.
fact: zelEnyi means green, the colour of grass and leaves.
fact: BIlyi means white and CHOrnyi means black.
fact: rOzhevyi means pink.
fact: pomarAnchevyi means orange, the colour; it comes from pomarAncha, an old word for the orange fruit, while oranges themselves are usually called apelsyny.
fact: fioLEtovyi means purple or violet.
fact: korYchnevyi means brown, like chocolate or a dog's coat.
fact: SIryi means grey, like a grey cat or a rainy day.
fact: yakoho kOlyoru? means what colour?, and the answer changes its ending, for example chervOnoho, red.
fact: The Ukrainian constitution describes the flag as two equal stripes, blue above and yellow below.
exercise: What does SYniy mean? | Dark blue | Light blue | Green | Grey
exercise: What does blakYtnyi mean? | Light blue, sky blue | Dark blue, like the night | Black | Purple
exercise: How many everyday words for blue does Ukrainian have? | Two: SYniy and blakYtnyi | One, like English | Three, one per shade | None, blue is borrowed from English
exercise: Why does calling the sky SYniy sound odd? | SYniy is dark blue, and the sky is light | SYniy means green | SYniy is a swear word | SYniy is only for sea water
exercise: What does ZHOvtyi mean? | Yellow | Orange | White | Gold only
exercise: What does zelEnyi mean? | Green | Sky blue | Yellow | Grey, dull
exercise: What does BIlyi mean? | White | Big, huge | Pitch black | Light blue
exercise: What does CHOrnyi mean? | Black | Brown | Dark blue | Grey
exercise: What does rOzhevyi mean? | Pink | Red | Rose-smelling | Orange
exercise: What does pomarAnchevyi mean? | Orange (the colour) | Purple | Lemon-yellow | Brown, like chocolate
exercise: Where does the word pomarAnchevyi come from? | pomarAncha, an old word for orange fruit | Pomeranian dogs | A town called Pomar | The word for tomato
exercise: What does fioLEtovyi mean? | Purple, violet | Pink | Dark blue, navy | Grey
exercise: What does korYchnevyi mean? | Brown | Black | Orange | Red
exercise: What does SIryi mean? | Grey | Serious | Silver only | White
exercise: What does yakoho kOlyoru? mean? | What colour? | Which one? | How many? | Whose is it?
exercise: How does the law describe the colours of the Ukrainian flag? | Blue above and yellow below | Yellow above and blue below | Red and black | Blue, yellow and white
test: «blue is blue, Ukrainians just have one word like us» Which is right? | No, they have two: SYniy dark, blakYtnyi light | Yes, only SYniy | Yes, only blakYtnyi | No, they have five blues
test: Your jeans are dark navy. Which word fits best? | SYniy | blakYtnyi | fioLEtovyi | SIryi
test: Your friend's T-shirt is pale like the summer sky. Which word fits best? | blakYtnyi | SYniy | CHOrnyi | korYchnevyi
test: «the flag's yellow on top, blue at the bottom» Which is right? | No, blue on top and yellow below | Yes, yellow on top | It is green and yellow, like grass | It is blue on the left
test: «pomarAnchevyi has orange in it so the fruit is pomarAncha in shops» What is right? | In shops oranges are usually apelsyny; pomarAncha is the old word | Yes, every shop says pomarAncha | The fruit is called pomarAnchevyi | The fruit is called ZHOvtyi
test: Sashko's sister holds up a crayon and asks «yakoho kOlyoru?». It's grass-coloured. What do you say? | zelEnoho | chervOnoho | SYnyoho | BIloho
test: Mix BIlyi paint with CHOrnyi paint. What colour do you get? | SIryi | rOzhevyi | korYchnevyi | ZHOvtyi
test: Mix chervOnyi and BIlyi paint. What do you get? | rOzhevyi | fioLEtovyi | SIryi | zelEnyi
test: A Ukrainian kid calls a light-blue and a dark-blue pencil «two different colours». Why is that sensible in his language? | Because SYniy and blakYtnyi are separate basic words, like red and pink | Because he is colour-blind | Because the pencils are different brands | Because blue is not a colour in Ukrainian
test: «grandad calls the flag zhovto-blakYtnyi but the law says SYniy, so grandad's wrong» Which is right? | No, zhovto-blakYtnyi is a very common, accepted way to say it | Yes, he is breaking the law | Yes, the flag is really green | No, the law says CHOrnyi
test: «brown is CHOrnyi innit, like dark» Which is right? | No, brown is korYchnevyi; CHOrnyi is black | Yes, brown is CHOrnyi | Brown is SIryi | Brown is rOzhevyi, a bit like pink and red mixed
test: The rainy-day sky is not blue at all. Which colour word fits? | SIryi | blakYtnyi | ZHOvtyi | rOzhevyi
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression of the colour blue in Ukrainian? | Two distinct basic terms are employed: SYniy for the dark and blakYtnyi for the light shade | A single term embraces all shades, as in English | Blue is expressed solely by a word borrowed from English | blakYtnyi denotes dark blue, whereas SYniy denotes light blue
exam: State the arrangement of the colours of the Ukrainian flag as described in its constitution. | Blue above and yellow below, in two equal stripes | Yellow above and blue below, in two equal stripes | Blue, yellow and white in three stripes | Yellow upon a blue field with a star
exam: Identify the derivation of the colour term pomarAnchevyi. | From pomarAncha, an older word for the orange fruit | From the name of a breed of small fluffy dog from Pomerania | From the word for tomato | From a French word for apple
exam: Ascertain the colour obtained by the admixture of BIlyi and CHOrnyi paint in equal measure. | SIryi | rOzhevyi | korYchnevyi | blakYtnyi
